<h1 class="!mb-0 no-underline">My toddler has a store. Stripe runs the payments. The process beat the model.</h1>
<p class="text-xl text-gray-300 !mt-2 !mb-6">Spec-driven development, round two — the cheap model was the proof, not the cause</p>
August 2026
<ProjectLinkButton href={projectUrl} />

<!-- IMAGE PLACEHOLDER H1: Hero shot — full setup: iPad on stand + Tera Mini scanner + Stripe Reader M2 + toys with barcodes -->

## Introduction

A toddler selling toys with real payment hardware — that's the fun part. The engineering story is the process behind it: the direct follow-up to my [Spec Kit article](/projects/speckit-dev), and the place where spec-driven development finally worked. Crucially, it worked *without* a cleverer model. This project ran on a cheaper, less sophisticated one than last time, at roughly a thirtieth of the price per token — and the work came out straighter. Better process, cheaper model, better result. Process beat model.

Two workflow changes did the work, and both are transferable. The first: my specs now feed **validated, structured artifacts** — state diagrams, message flows, UI mocks — instead of jumping straight to a plan. The second: the whole stack, a Rust backend and a Swift iPad app, lived in **one devcontainer I could drive from my phone**, with a feedback loop measured in minutes and run on live toddler feedback. That's what this post is about.

Here's the setup, because it's worth setting the scene: an iPad on a stand, a wireless barcode scanner, and a [Stripe Reader M2](https://stripe.com/terminal) on our kitchen table. My three-year-old scans a toy, taps a card, and the app throws a celebration. Underneath is a real payments stack — Stripe's Terminal SDK, a Rust worker on Cloudflare, KV for inventory, D1 for the transaction log — and a build process that finally held the line.

## The Toy Store, Meet Stripe

<!-- IMAGE PLACEHOLDER H2: Hardware spread — the "meet Stripe" table: scanner, M2, test cards, iPad stand -->

The scene: an iPad on a stand, a Tera Mini barcode scanner, a Stripe Reader M2, a small stack of test cards, and a shelf of toys wearing printed barcode labels. The product owner is three years old. The QA lead is three years old. The most demanding user is three years old.

Here's the flow. She picks up the scanner, points it at a toy, and pulls the trigger. The iPad beeps, and the item appears on screen: a big photo, its name, and a price. Prices are deliberately integer — $1, $5, no cents, no tax, no shipping, no surprises. Each scan adds to a running total with a satisfying chime. When she's ready, she taps a card on the M2, the reader flashes green, and then the app lets loose: fireworks, bouncing images of everything she just sold, sound, and on a good day a little video of a guitar band playing. Then "Go Again" and the cart resets.

<!-- IMAGE PLACEHOLDER H3–H7: iPad POS states — idle → item added → card present → celebration → "Go Again" (grid or carousel) -->
<!-- IMAGE PLACEHOLDER H8: Toys + barcodes — close-up of printed Avery barcode labels on toys -->
<!-- IMAGE PLACEHOLDER H9: M2 + test cards — close-up of the Reader M2 and Stripe test cards -->

One design principle guided all of it: **transactions should always succeed.** This is a toy, not a fraud engine. If the capture fails for a boring reason, the sale still happens — the toddler never sees a "payment declined" screen. That single decision kept the whole product honest about what it is, and it quietly shaped the architecture: the celebration is the source of truth for the toddler, and the ledger is there to keep me honest, not to police her.

## The System

Three pieces, and each one is deliberately conventional:

**The iPad app (Swift).** Minimal text, big buttons, bright colors, celebratory animations. The scanner connects over Bluetooth in keyboard-wedge mode — the app just sees keystrokes ending in a carriage return and parses the barcode from them. The M2 connects through the [Stripe Terminal iOS SDK](https://docs.stripe.com/terminal). Every request to the backend is signed with Apple App Attest and an API key generated from [fintechnick.com/api-keys](/api-keys), so only my iPad can talk to the worker at all.

**The backend (Rust on Cloudflare Workers).** One monolithic worker. It issues Terminal connection tokens, creates `PaymentIntent`s with the `card_present` payment method type, and captures payments once the card is authorized. Inventory lives in [KV](https://developers.cloudflare.com/kv/) keyed by barcode for edge-speed lookups; every completed sale is logged to [D1](https://developers.cloudflare.com/d1/) for analytics. I'd never written Rust before this project — a big part of the fun was discovering that Workers' Rust support is genuinely pleasant.

**The admin side.** Inventory management: photo upload, pricing, barcode generation, and printing onto [Avery 1" × 2-5/8" address labels](https://www.avery.com/) using nothing more exotic than CSS `@media print` rules. Plus a transaction log for analytics. The backend does the heavy lifting; the admin frontend lives on this site at [/projects/stripe-toddler-admin](/projects/stripe-toddler-admin).

Here's the component graph straight out of the specs:

<div class="mermaid-container">

```mermaid
graph TD
    subgraph ClientDevices [Client Devices and Peripherals]
        iPadApp["iPad POS App&#10;Swift / iOS"]
        BarcodeScanner["Tera Mini Barcode Scanner&#10;Bluetooth HID Mode"]
        StripeReader["Stripe Reader M2&#10;Bluetooth BLE Mode"]
        AveryPrinter["Avery Label Printer&#10;AirPrint / Browser Print"]
    end

    subgraph ExternalServices [External Integrations]
        StripeAPI["Stripe API&#10;Terminal and Payments"]
        AppAttest["Apple App Attest Service&#10;Device Verification"]
    end

    subgraph EdgeBackend [Cloudflare Workers Edge Platform]
        RustWorker["Payments and Admin API Worker&#10;Rust / Wasm"]
        subgraph CloudflareStorage [Cloudflare Storage]
            D1Db["D1 Relational DB&#10;SQLite Transactions"]
            KVStore["KV Namespace&#10;Inventory Data"]
        end
    end

    subgraph AdminControl [Admin Portal]
        AdminUI["Admin Web App&#10;www.fintechnick.com"]
    end

    BarcodeScanner -->|Bluetooth HID / Keystrokes| iPadApp
    StripeReader <-->|Bluetooth BLE / Stripe Terminal SDK| iPadApp
    iPadApp <-->|HTTPS REST / App Attest Signed| RustWorker
    AdminUI <-->|HTTPS REST / Auth API Key| RustWorker

    RustWorker <-->|Cloudflare Binding| D1Db
    RustWorker <-->|Cloudflare Binding| KVStore

    RustWorker <-->|HTTPS / REST API| StripeAPI
    RustWorker -->|HTTPS / Validation Requests| AppAttest
    AdminUI -->|Browser Render / CSS @media print| AveryPrinter
```

</div>

The worker's [OpenAPI spec](https://github.com/nickbrett1/stripe-toddler/blob/main/specs/spec/api/worker-openapi.yaml) was one of the artifacts we validated up front. Here's the entire POS surface — nine endpoints, three of them the whole payments flow:

```yaml
# worker-openapi.yaml (excerpt) — the POS endpoints
paths:
  /api/pos/inventory/{barcode}:
    get:
      summary: Look up an item by barcode
      description: Used by the iPad POS app. Secured by App Attest assertion headers.
  /api/terminal/connection-token:
    post:
      summary: Get Stripe Terminal connection token
      description: Contacts Stripe to generate a short-lived connection token
        so the iPad can authorize a session with the Reader M2.
  /api/terminal/payment-intent:
    post:
      summary: Create PaymentIntent
      description: Creates a card_present PaymentIntent for the cart total.
  /api/terminal/capture:
    post:
      summary: Capture PaymentIntent
      description: Captures the authorized PaymentIntent and logs the transaction to D1.
```

The data split is boring in the best possible way — each store answers the one question it's good at:

```json
// KV — inventory, keyed by barcode, read at the edge on every scan
{
  "pattern": "item:<barcode>",
  "example_key": "item:071234567890",
  "schema": {
    "required": ["barcode", "name", "price_cents", "image_url", "created_at"]
  }
}
```

```sql
-- D1 — the transaction log, relational, for analytics
CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS transactions (
    transaction_id TEXT PRIMARY KEY NOT NULL,
    payment_intent_id TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE,
    amount_cents INTEGER NOT NULL,
    status TEXT NOT NULL,
    created_at INTEGER NOT NULL
);

CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS transaction_items (
    item_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,
    transaction_id TEXT NOT NULL,
    barcode TEXT NOT NULL,
    name TEXT NOT NULL,
    price_cents INTEGER NOT NULL,
    quantity INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 1,
    FOREIGN KEY (transaction_id) REFERENCES transactions (transaction_id) ON DELETE CASCADE
);
```

## The Process: Spec-Driven Development, Round Two

This is the section that matters, so let me be honest about where I started. In the [Spec Kit article](/projects/speckit-dev) I described what failed: cost blowups from forward-only iteration, specs that were hard to update once code existed, and giant single-branch merges at the end. Even on a flagship model — Gemini Pro at the time — the agent wandered. Two changes fixed that, and they're the real subject of this article.

### Change #1 — a structured artifact pipeline

The pipeline is now **spec → artifacts → validation → plan**, not spec → plan. And it's codified as public [goose recipes](https://github.com/nickbrett1/goose-recipes) in `github.com/nickbrett1/goose-recipes`, so it's reproducible and improvable:

1. **design-project** — a clarifying-questions interview that produces `description.md`, the prose spec. The governing principle: this is a *design doc, not a full coding spec*. A senior engineer reading it should feel confident they could implement the whole thing, making judgment calls on the details.
2. **Artifact generation** — recipes per artifact type produce structured artifacts: state diagrams, message flows, UI mocks, plus topology, API, data, and capacity models.
3. **validate-spec** — artifacts are cross-checked for consistency against a dependency map (**spec-dag**) *before anything is built*. This is the step that kills the tangents: the agent implements a consistent, validated artifact set instead of improvising from prose.
4. **generate-plan** — the plan is generated *off the validated artifacts*. It's a translation of agreed structure, not a first draft.
5. **scaffold-project + execute-plan** — once validated, the repo itself is scaffolded via genproj (devcontainers, CircleCI, Doppler, Sonar, the lot), then implementation runs phase-by-phase in-repo.

Here's the artifact tree this produced for stripe-toddler — every file cross-checked against the others:

```text
spec/
├── topology/
│   ├── component-graph.mmd        # system nodes and DAG
│   └── deployment-targets.md      # infrastructure mapping
├── api/
│   ├── worker-openapi.yaml        # the Rust worker's API
│   └── swift-protocols.md         # iOS interface contracts
├── data-architecture/
│   ├── d1-schema.sql              # SQLite analytics schema
│   ├── kv-layout.json             # inventory access patterns
│   └── domain-models.rs           # core structs
├── flows/
│   ├── checkout-sequence.md       # step-by-step transaction walkthrough
│   └── payment-state-machine.md   # intent and hardware lifecycle
├── ui/
│   └── wireframes/                # low-fidelity Excalidraw mocks
└── capacity/
    └── load-model.md              # sizing, retention, IOPS
```

The payoff was visible immediately. Here's the payment state machine — a validated artifact that both I and the agent could hold in our heads while building:

<div class="mermaid-container">

```mermaid
stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> WaitingForScan : App launch and initialization

    WaitingForScan --> CartActive : Barcode scanned and verified in KV
    WaitingForScan --> ErrorScreen : Network error or barcode not in KV

    CartActive --> CartActive : Additional items scanned
    CartActive --> WaitingForScan : All items removed from cart
    CartActive --> ReaderSyncing : Tap the Pay button

    ReaderSyncing --> AwaitingCardTap : Terminal token retrieved and intent created
    ReaderSyncing --> ErrorScreen : Stripe API error or backend timeout

    AwaitingCardTap --> ProcessingPayment : Card tapped successfully
    AwaitingCardTap --> ErrorScreen : Card read timeout or card rejected

    ProcessingPayment --> Celebrating : Capture endpoint returns HTTP 200
    ProcessingPayment --> ErrorScreen : Capture fails on Stripe or database write error

    Celebrating --> WaitingForScan : Tap the Go Again button
    ErrorScreen --> WaitingForScan : Tap the Dismiss button
```

</div>

And here's the checkout flow as a message sequence, again from the specs — this is the diagram that made the whole system feel tractable before a line of Swift or Rust existed:

<div class="mermaid-container">

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    actor Toddler as Toddler
    participant iPad as iPad App (Swift)
    participant Scanner as Barcode Scanner (HID)
    participant Reader as Stripe Reader M2 (BLE)
    participant Backend as Rust Worker (Cloudflare)
    participant KV as Cloudflare KV
    participant D1 as D1 SQLite DB
    participant Stripe as Stripe API

    Note over Toddler, Scanner: Scan phase
    Toddler->>Scanner: Scans toy barcode
    Scanner->>iPad: Keystrokes and carriage return
    iPad->>Backend: GET inventory by barcode (attested)
    Backend->>KV: Fetch item details
    KV-->>Backend: Name, price, photo URL
    Backend-->>iPad: Item details

    Note over Toddler, Reader: Payment phase
    Toddler->>iPad: Taps the Pay button
    iPad->>Backend: POST payment-intent
    Backend->>Stripe: POST /v1/payment_intents (card_present)
    Stripe-->>Backend: client_secret and intent ID
    Backend-->>iPad: client_secret and intent ID
    iPad->>Reader: collectPaymentMethod(client_secret)
    Toddler->>Reader: Taps test card
    Reader-->>iPad: Authorized intent

    Note over iPad, D1: Capture phase
    iPad->>Backend: POST capture
    Backend->>Stripe: POST /v1/payment_intents/pi_xxx/capture
    Stripe-->>Backend: Capture succeeded
    Backend->>D1: INSERT INTO transactions
    D1-->>Backend: Confirmed
    Backend-->>iPad: Transaction ID

    Note over Toddler, iPad: Celebration phase
    iPad->>iPad: Fireworks, guitar band, sound
    Toddler->>iPad: Taps Go Again
```

</div>

The point isn't the diagrams themselves — it's that they existed *before* the code, agreed and cross-checked. When something did need to change, it rippled through small, cheap artifacts instead of through a mountain of generated code. That was the exact failure mode from last time, addressed at the root.

### Change #2 — iteration infrastructure

The second change is the tooling that made iteration cheap enough to do constantly:

- **One devcontainer, one view.** The Rust backend and the Swift iPad app both live in a single VSCode devcontainer. Xcode is driven through its MCP server, bridged from inside the container by a small proxy ([`mcp-sse-proxy.cjs`](https://github.com/nickbrett1/stripe-toddler/blob/main/.agents/mcp-sse-proxy.cjs) → `xcode-native` MCP), so the agent can edit, rebuild, and run the iOS tests without ever leaving the container. No context-switching between an IDE and a backend toolchain.

```json
// .agents/mcp_config.json (excerpt) — the bridge that makes the magic chain possible
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "xcode-native": {
      "command": "node",
      "args": [
        "/workspaces/stripe-toddler/.agents/mcp-sse-proxy.cjs",
        "http://mac-studio:9876/sse"
      ]
    }
  }
}
```

- **Phone access.** [Blink Shell](https://blink.sh/) + mosh into the devcontainer from my phone over Tailscale — each devcontainer runs Tailscale itself, so the phone connects *directly* to the container, no host hop. Iteration happens anywhere, including standing next to the toddler.
- **LiteLLM.** A single switch to route to cheaper models. This project ran on **DeepSeek-v4-flash** at published rates of **$0.14/$0.28 per M tokens** (input/output), instead of the Gemini Pro that was current when I wrote the December 2025 article — **Gemini 3 Pro at $2.50/$10.00 per M tokens**. That's roughly 18× cheaper on input and 36× cheaper on output. Agentic work skews output-heavy, so the real-world delta landed around **30×**. Cheap had its own compounding benefit too: no rate-limit anxiety on a subscription, which made me willing to iterate more aggressively.

The guardrails carried over from last time: `.agents/.rules` with [git and testing guidelines](https://github.com/nickbrett1/stripe-toddler/tree/main/.agents/.rules), plus SonarCloud, GitGuardian, and Dependabot in CI.

The pitch, in one line: this is the iteration story the Spec Kit article said was missing — validate artifacts *before* generating code, so changes ripple cheaply, and the model becomes almost an implementation detail.

## The Magical Moment

<!-- IMAGE PLACEHOLDER M1: Phone screenshot — live Blink + mosh session into the devcontainer -->

First real session. The toddler is the user, the demo is happening live, and the post-purchase celebration — the part I was most proud of — isn't exciting enough for her. She watches the fireworks with the flat, professional disappointment only a three-year-old can deliver.

Here's the chain, blow by blow:

1. **Phone** — [Blink Shell](https://blink.sh/), mosh straight into the VSCode devcontainer over Tailscale. Each container runs its own Tailscale, so it's a direct connection; mosh keeps the session alive while I walk around the room.
2. **Agent** — I ask the agent to make the celebration more exciting.
3. **One workspace** — the devcontainer holds the whole stack: the Rust backend and the Swift iPad app. No toolchain switch, no new terminal, no context loss.
4. **MCP bridge** — the agent reaches the custom MCP wrapper on the Mac host (`mcp-sse-proxy.cjs` → `xcode-native` MCP at `mac-studio:9876/sse`).
5. **Xcode** — the wrapper drives Xcode end-to-end: edit → rebuild → run tests, all automated, no step-by-step approval.
6. **The iPad, 10 meters away** — reloads with the new celebration, mid-demo, in response to observed user feedback.

In a pre-agentic world, that's a "note it down and fix it tonight" moment — a task that goes on a list, gets picked up later, and ships tomorrow at the earliest. Now it's a **2-minute loop**: observation → instruction → shipped → verified, all while the user is watching. That loop, more than any single feature, was the product.

This was also the first real end-to-end use of the iPhone → devcontainer agent pipeline — a share-to-agent idea I'd sketched a while back, finally exercised for real. Worth one line because it stopped being a sketch.

## Takeaways

Three things, and I'll keep them to three:

1. **Artifacts before code.** The missing step from the first spec-kit project: typed, cross-validated artifacts — state diagrams, message flows, UI mocks — sitting between the spec and the plan. Spec-dag validation is what stopped the wandering. Show a little to explain it, but the full [artifact set is public](https://github.com/nickbrett1/stripe-toddler/tree/main/specs/spec), as are the [recipes](https://github.com/nickbrett1/goose-recipes) that produce it.
2. **Process beats model.** The process got good enough that a cheaper, less capable model was plenty: DeepSeek-v4-flash at ~$0.14/$0.28 per M tokens produced straighter work than last time's flagship at ~$2.50/$10.00. Roughly 30× cheaper and visibly better behaved — and without the subscription rate-limit anxiety, iterating got more ambitious, not less.
3. **Make iteration a phone-speed loop.** Devcontainer + MCP + mosh turned "note it down and fix it tonight" into a 2-minute loop. The loop was the real product — it's why a three-year-old's feedback could drive the build in real time.

